  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An area of low pressure is progged to develop\nalong a stalled front off the east coast of FL later today and\ntonight, then lift N or NNEward Sat through Sun. There remains\nconsiderable uncertainly with the timing, track and strength of\nthe low as it lifts across the region but hopefully guidance \nwill come into better agreement once the low develops. Latest \nblended model guidance has shifted the track westward a bit with\nmore trajectory over land and therefore is a bit weaker than \nthe previous forecast. \n\nWhile exact impacts remain in flux, the greatest impacts are\nexpected along the coast where onshore flow, large seas and high\nastronomical tides will bring a threat for large surf, beach\nerosion, and possible ocean overwash. Confidence is less for\ncoastal flooding impacts across the sounds and rivers,\nespecially as the forecasted winds across the sounds had\ndiminished, but given the developing King Tides, there may be\nminor inundation for locations adjacent to the sounds and \nrivers.\n\nWe do expect this system to bring beneficial rainfall across \nthe region with widespread accumulation around 1-2\" from \nSaturday through Sunday night. However, drought conditions have \nworsened across ENC with the update that came out yesterday with\nthe expansion of severe and extreme drought conditions across \nthe area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...King Tide Cycle starts second half of this \nweekend, peaking middle of next week. This higher than normal \nastronomical tide cycle is slightly stronger than the one that \noccurred the last week of OCT. The tide levels may be \nexacerbated by low pressure systems that are expected to impact \nthe region, with the first this weekend with another potential \nlow pressure system around the middle of next week. The\nstrength and track of the lows will determine where the greatest\nimpacts will occur, but most tidal locations across ENC should\nexpect elevated water levels with at least minor impacts across\nthe typical vulnerable areas. Interests along the coast should\nwatch the forecast closely over the next week for the potential\nfor greater impacts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...In the wake of this weekend's low, a high\nbriefly builds in early next week. Models continue to show a \ndeepening trough moving through the Gulf states and progressing \noffshore of the SE US. This shortwave may encounter a stationary\nboundary offshore of SE US and the baroclinic zone along the \nGulf Stream and produce a sfc short wave or low. The pressure \ngradient would tighten considerably in this scenario, with the \nhigh sitting over the NE. Confidence remains low at this point, \nbut if materialized, we could see a period of strong winds,\nelevated seas, coastal flooding impacts coinciding with the \npeak of King Tides, and periods of heavy rainfall. This system\nwill also have the potential to bring impacts to holiday travel\non Wednesday and Thursday.",
